  "question": {
    "text": "Given that $K=$ energy, $V=$ velocity, $T=$ time. If they are chosen as the fundamental units, then what is dimensional formula for surface tension?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "AuBac",
      "text": "\n$\\left[K V^{-2} \\mathrm{~T}^{-2}\\right]$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "YfCLH",
      "text": "\n$\\left[K^2 V^2 T^{-2}\\right]$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "wCYrI",
      "text": "\n$\\left[K^2 V^{-2} T^{-2}\\right]$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "qMsNK",
      "text": "\n$\\left[K V^2 T^2\\right]$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Surface tension $=\\frac{F}{\\ell}=\\frac{F}{\\ell} \\cdot \\frac{\\ell}{\\ell} \\cdot \\frac{T^2}{T^2}$ (As, $F . \\ell=K\\left(\\right.$ energy) $; \\frac{T^2}{\\ell^2}=V^{-2}$ ) Therefore, surface tension $=\\left[K V^{-2} T^{-2}\\right]$",
    "image": null
  },
